# Go Testing Practices

For the full TDD workflow (Red-Green-Refactor), see `test-driven-development.mdc`.

## Black Box Testing (Required)

**Always use black box testing.** Test files must use an external test package (`package <name>_test`), import the package under test, and exercise only its **public API** (exported types, functions, and methods).

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Black box test — external test package, public API only
package domain_test

import (
    "testing"

    "github.com/pedromsmoreira/rewards-center/internal/domain"
    "github.com/stretchr/testify/assert"
)

func TestNewEmail_WithValidEmail_ReturnsEmail(t *testing.T) {
    t.Run("creates email successfully", func(t *testing.T) {
        e, err := domain.NewEmail("user@example.com")
        assert.NoError(t, err)
        assert.Equal(t, "user@example.com", e.Value())
    })
}

// ❌ BAD: White box test — same package as implementation
package domain

func TestNewEmail_WithValidEmail_ReturnsEmail(t *testing.T) {
    e, err := NewEmail("user@example.com") // accesses unexported internals easily
    // ...
}

// ❌ BAD: External package but testing unexported symbols
package domain_test

func Test_parseEmail(t *testing.T) {
    // parseEmail is unexported — forbidden
}
```

**Rules:**

- **Required**: `package <dirname>_test` (e.g. `config_test` for `internal/config`, `http_test` for `internal/handler/http`)
- **Required**: Import the package under test with its module path; reference exported symbols via the import prefix (e.g. `config.Load()`, `httphandler.Liveness()`)
- **Forbidden**: `package foo` in `foo_test.go` when testing package `foo` (white box)
- **Forbidden**: Accessing unexported identifiers from the package under test
- **Why**: Tests document and verify the public contract; refactors of internals don't break tests unless behavior changes

If a behavior cannot be observed through the public API, expose it via the public API (or redesign) rather than switching to white box tests.

## Test Structure

- Use `testify` for assertions
- **Main test functions MUST contain subtests using `t.Run`**
- Each test case should be a subtest within the main test function
- Use individual test functions with descriptive names for different behaviors/scenarios

### Main Test Function Pattern

**Rule**: Every main test function (`TestXxx`) should contain one or more subtests using `t.Run`. The main test function acts as a container for related test cases.

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Main test function contains subtests (black box)
package domain_test

func TestNewEmail_WithValidEmail_ReturnsEmail(t *testing.T) {
    t.Run("creates email successfully", func(t *testing.T) {
        e, err := domain.NewEmail("user@example.com")
        assert.NoError(t, err)
        assert.Equal(t, "user@example.com", e.Value())
    })
}

// ✅ GOOD: Main test function with multiple subtests for related scenarios
func TestNewEmail_WithInvalidInput_ReturnsError(t *testing.T) {
    t.Run("returns error for invalid email format", func(t *testing.T) {
        _, err := domain.NewEmail("not-an-email")
        assert.ErrorIs(t, err, domain.ErrInvalidEmail)
    })
    
    t.Run("returns error for empty string", func(t *testing.T) {
        _, err := domain.NewEmail("")
        assert.ErrorIs(t, err, domain.ErrInvalidEmail)
    })
    
    t.Run("returns error for missing @ symbol", func(t *testing.T) {
        _, err := domain.NewEmail("userexample.com")
        assert.ErrorIs(t, err, domain.ErrInvalidEmail)
    })
}

// ❌ BAD: Test function without subtests
func TestNewEmail_WithValidEmail_ReturnsEmail(t *testing.T) {
    e, err := domain.NewEmail("user@example.com")
    assert.NoError(t, err)
    assert.Equal(t, "user@example.com", e.Value())
}

// ❌ BAD: Multiple separate test functions for related scenarios
func TestNewEmail_InvalidFormat(t *testing.T) { /* ... */ }
func TestNewEmail_EmptyString(t *testing.T) { /* ... */ }
func TestNewEmail_MissingAtSymbol(t *testing.T) { /* ... */ }
// Should be combined into one test function with subtests
```

## Unit Tests

- Test one thing at a time
- Use mocks for dependencies
- Test behavior, not implementation
- Keep tests fast (< 100ms each)

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Unit test with mock — black box, public constructor
package service_test

import (
    "context"
    "testing"

    "github.com/pedromsmoreira/rewards-center/internal/service"
    "github.com/pedromsmoreira/rewards-center/internal/service/mocks"
    "github.com/pedromsmoreira/rewards-center/pkg/errors"
    "github.com/pedromsmoreira/rewards-center/test/fixtures"
    "github.com/stretchr/testify/assert"
    "github.com/stretchr/testify/require"
    "go.uber.org/mock/gomock"
)

func TestUserService_GetUser(t *testing.T) {
    t.Run("returns user when found", func(t *testing.T) {
        ctrl := gomock.NewController(t)

        want := fixtures.NewUser(t, fixtures.WithID("123"))
        mockRepo := mocks.NewMockUserRepository(ctrl)
        mockRepo.EXPECT().
            GetByID(gomock.Any(), "123").
            Return(want, nil)

        svc := service.NewUserService(mockRepo)
        user, err := svc.GetUser(context.Background(), "123")

        require.NoError(t, err)
        assert.Equal(t, "123", user.ID())
    })

    t.Run("returns error when user not found", func(t *testing.T) {
        ctrl := gomock.NewController(t)

        mockRepo := mocks.NewMockUserRepository(ctrl)
        mockRepo.EXPECT().
            GetByID(gomock.Any(), "999").
            Return(nil, errors.ErrNotFound)

        svc := service.NewUserService(mockRepo)
        user, err := svc.GetUser(context.Background(), "999")

        assert.ErrorIs(t, err, errors.ErrNotFound)
        assert.Nil(t, user)
    })
}
```

## Mocking

- Use **gomock** with mocks generated from interfaces (e.g. mockgen). Set expectations using the **method**, not the name.
- **Required**: `ctrl := gomock.NewController(t)`; generated mock (e.g. `mocks.NewMockUserRepository(ctrl)`); expectations via `mock.EXPECT().GetByID(...).Return(...)`.
- **Forbidden**: String-based method expectations (e.g. `mock.On("GetByID", ...)`). No hand-written mocks that use `On("MethodName", ...)`.
- **Why**: Renaming an interface method (e.g. `GetByID` → `FindByID`) causes compile errors at every `EXPECT().GetByID(...)`; you update tests in one pass. With `On("GetByID", ...)`, refactors don't break the build and tests become brittle.

```go
// ❌ BAD: String-based expectations — forbidden
mockRepo.On("GetByID", mock.Anything, "123").Return(user, nil)
// Use gomock with EXPECT().GetByID(gomock.Any(), "123") instead.
```

## Integration Tests

There are two types of integration tests in this project:

### 1. Service-Level Integration Tests (in `internal/`)

For testing service + repository + database without HTTP:

- Use `testcontainers-go` for database
- Test files next to source code (`*_test.go`)
- Use shared test database
- Clean up after tests

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Service-level integration test (in internal/) — black box
package repository_test

import (
    "context"
    "testing"

    "github.com/pedromsmoreira/rewards-center/internal/repository"
    "github.com/stretchr/testify/assert"
    "github.com/stretchr/testify/require"
)

func TestEntityRepository_Create_WithValidEntity_PersistsToDatabase(t *testing.T) {
    t.Run("persists entity and retrieves it", func(t *testing.T) {
        ctx := context.Background()
        db := setupTestDB(t)
        defer db.Close()
        
        repo := repository.NewEntityRepository(db)
        
        entity, err := repo.Create(ctx, testEntity)
        require.NoError(t, err)
        
        found, err := repo.GetByID(ctx, entity.ID())
        require.NoError(t, err)
        assert.Equal(t, entity.ID(), found.ID())
    })
}
```

### 2. E2E/API Integration Tests (in `test/`)

For testing complete HTTP workflows with BDD stage patterns:

> **Important**: E2E tests in the `test/` folder **must** follow `e2e-testing-standards.mdc` for Given-When-Then architecture, stage files, and HTTP/event testing.

```go
// ✅ GOOD: E2E test using BDD stage pattern (in test/)
func TestCreateEntity(t *testing.T) {
    t.Run("when creating an entity returns 201 created", func(t *testing.T) {
        given, when, then := newCreateEntityStage(t)
        
        given.aCreateEntityRequestIsPrepared()
        when.createEntityEndpointIsCalled()
        then.responseShouldHaveStatusCode(http.StatusCreated)
    })
}
```

## Test Naming

- Format: `Test<Function>_<Scenario>_<ExpectedResult>` for main test functions
- Be descriptive about what is being tested
- Use subtests for multiple related scenarios within the same main test function
- Use separate main test functions for different behaviors or distinct test groups

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Descriptive test names with subtests
func TestUser_ChangeEmail_WithValidEmail_UpdatesEmail(t *testing.T) {
    t.Run("updates email successfully", func(t *testing.T) {
        // test implementation
    })
    
    t.Run("updates updated_at timestamp", func(t *testing.T) {
        // test implementation
    })
}

func TestUser_ChangeEmail_WithInvalidInput_ReturnsError(t *testing.T) {
    t.Run("returns error for same email", func(t *testing.T) {
        // test implementation
    })
    
    t.Run("returns error for invalid email format", func(t *testing.T) {
        // test implementation
    })
}

func TestEntityService_Create_WithUnauthorizedUser_ReturnsForbidden(t *testing.T) {
    t.Run("returns forbidden when user lacks permission", func(t *testing.T) {
        // test implementation
    })
}
```

## Test Organization

### File Structure

- Test files next to source: `user_test.go` with `package user_test` (black box)
- E2E tests in `test/` (NOT `test/integration/`)
- Test utilities in `test/`
- Shared test helpers for a package may live in the test file or a `*_test.go` helper file in the **test package** (still `package user_test`)

### Test Categories

1. **Unit Tests**: Fast, isolated, mock dependencies
2. **Integration Tests**: Test multiple components together

## Test Coverage

- Maintain >80% code coverage
- Focus on critical paths (business logic, error handling)
- Don't chase 100% coverage (test quality > quantity)
- Use coverage reports to find untested code

## Best Practices

### DO

- ✅ Use black box testing (`package <name>_test`, public API only)
- ✅ Write tests before implementation (TDD)
- ✅ Test behavior, not implementation
- ✅ Keep tests independent and isolated
- ✅ Use descriptive test names
- ✅ Clean up test data
- ✅ Test error cases and edge cases
- ✅ Use mocks for external dependencies

### DON'T

- ❌ Use white box tests (`package foo` in `foo_test.go`)
- ❌ Access unexported symbols from the package under test
- ❌ Test implementation details
- ❌ Write tests that depend on other tests
- ❌ Ignore flaky tests (fix them)
- ❌ Write tests that are too complex
- ❌ Test framework code
- ❌ Skip error case testing
- ❌ Commit tests that don't pass

## Running Tests

```bash
go test ./...                    # All tests
go test -v ./internal/service    # Verbose output
go test -cover ./...             # With coverage
make test-integration            # Integration tests
```

## Test Data

- Use factories/builders for test data (e.g. a `test/fixtures` package)
- Build entities through domain constructors; entity fields are unexported, so factories can't (and shouldn't) bypass validation
- Keep test data realistic
- Use constants for shared test data
- Clean up test data after tests

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Test data factory with functional options, built via domain constructors
package fixtures

type userParams struct {
    id       string
    username string
    email    string
    userType domain.UserType
}

type UserOption func(*userParams)

func WithID(id string) UserOption {
    return func(p *userParams) { p.id = id }
}

func NewUser(t testing.TB, opts ...UserOption) *domain.User {
    t.Helper()

    p := userParams{
        id:       uuid.NewString(),
        username: "testuser",
        email:    "test@example.com",
        userType: domain.RoleX, // or appropriate role from domain
    }
    for _, opt := range opts {
        opt(&p)
    }

    email, err := domain.NewEmail(p.email)
    require.NoError(t, err)
    user, err := domain.NewUser(p.id, p.username, email, p.userType)
    require.NoError(t, err)
    return user
}

// ❌ BAD: Struct literal on an entity bypasses constructor validation
user := &domain.User{ID: "123", Email: "test@example.com"}
```
